-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Deliberately EXCLUDED from RLS — global / catalog tables, no tenant_id:
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
--   users              — identity only; tenant association lives in
--                         tenant_memberships, which IS RLS'd above.
--   subscription_plans — global plan catalog, same rows visible to everyone.
--   ares_cache          — public ARES registry data, identical regardless of
--                         which tenant looked it up.
--   tos_versions        — published VOP/ToS text, same for everyone.
--
-- If any of these ever gain a tenant_id column, add a matching policy above.
--
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Deliberately EXCLUDED from RLS — Auth.js (`@auth/drizzle-adapter`) tables:
-- accounts, sessions, verification_tokens (no tenant_id column at all):
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Same category of exemption as `users` immediately above — NOT the same
-- category as the `channel_links` exemption below. `users`/`accounts`/
-- `sessions`/`verification_tokens` are user-level identity data: a single
-- user can hold memberships in multiple tenants via `tenant_memberships`
-- (which DOES have a tenant_id and IS RLS'd), so "which tenant does this
-- OAuth account / session / magic-link token belong to" isn't a meaningful
-- question — the answer is "none, it belongs to a user, not a tenant."
-- There is no tenant_id column to write a policy against, and adding one
-- would misrepresent the data model rather than fix a security gap. These
-- four tables' access control is instead the adapter's own responsibility
-- (session tokens are unguessable bearer secrets; verification tokens are
-- single-use and short-lived) plus the app's session-to-request-context
-- wiring that resolves which tenant a signed-in *user* is currently acting
-- as. See the comment on `accounts`/`sessions`/`verificationTokens` in
-- `drizzle/schema.ts` for the same note colocated with the tables.
--
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Deliberately EXCLUDED from RLS — channel_links (DOES have a tenant_id
-- column, but is intentionally NOT RLS'd — read this before "fixing" it):
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- `channel_links` binds a Telegram/WhatsApp `external_user_id` to a tenant
-- (blueprint §6 step 2). Its whole purpose is to answer "which tenant does
-- this not-yet-authenticated external sender belong to?" — i.e. to resolve
-- a tenant_id that ISN'T known yet. A tenant_id-keyed RLS policy here would
-- make that resolution require already knowing the tenant_id it's supposed
-- to produce (`POST /api/v1/channel-links/verify` would need
-- `app.current_tenant_id` set before it could look up... the tenant_id) —
-- a chicken-and-egg deadlock, not a security boundary. Unlike
-- invoices/contacts/etc., `channel_links` is a lookup/index table, not
-- tenant-owned business data, so tenant-scoped row access isn't actually
-- its security model. Its real protections are:
--   - `linking_token`: single-use in intent, short-lived, required to
--     establish a row before it can ever be looked up by external_user_id;
--   - `external_user_id` values being platform-issued ids (Telegram/
--     WhatsApp) that are unguessable in practice, combined with the unique
--     index on `(channel, external_user_id)` in `drizzle/schema.ts`
--     guaranteeing at most one tenant is ever resolvable per sender.
-- See `apps/backend/src/channel-links/channel-links.service.ts` (both
-- `verify()` and `createLinkingToken()`) for how tenant scoping is instead
-- enforced at the application layer for this one table. Do not add
-- `ALTER TABLE channel_links ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY` back without solving
-- the chicken-and-egg problem above first.

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- SECURITY DEFINER lookup functions — resolving a tenant by external billing
-- id BEFORE app.current_tenant_id is known (TenantBillingRepository)
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- `TenantBillingRepository.findTenantIdByStripeCustomerId` /
-- `findTenantIdByGopayPayerId` (apps/backend/src/billing/tenant-billing.
-- repository.ts) hit the same chicken-and-egg shape as `channel_links`
-- above: a Stripe/GoPay webhook only carries an external identifier
-- (`stripe_customer_id` / `gopay_payer_id`), and the entire point of the
-- lookup is to discover which tenant that identifier belongs to — there is
-- no `app.current_tenant_id` to set yet, because the tenant isn't known
-- yet. `tenants` has `FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY` (above), so a plain pooled
-- SELECT with no tenant context set matches zero rows against a real
-- RLS-enabled Postgres instance.
--
-- IMPORTANT: this is NOT the `channel_links` fix. `tenants` legitimately
-- holds sensitive per-tenant data (company/legal name, IČO/DIČ, address,
-- bank account, subscription status) that must stay isolated between
-- tenants — dropping RLS from the whole table the way `channel_links` did
-- would let any query read every tenant's row, which is exactly the leak
-- RLS exists to prevent. Instead, `tenants` keeps `ENABLE`/`FORCE ROW LEVEL
-- SECURITY` fully intact, and we open exactly one narrow, purpose-built
-- door through it.
--
-- A `SECURITY DEFINER` function runs with the privileges of the role that
-- OWNS the function (the migration/table-owning role that runs this
-- script — see the runtime-role note at the top of this file), not the
-- privileges of the caller (`faktur_app`). That's what lets it see across
-- tenants despite the caller having no `app.current_tenant_id` set. That is
-- a real, deliberate privilege escalation, so it is scoped as tightly as
-- possible:
--   - it returns ONLY the `id` column (a single uuid) — never company
--     name, IČO/DIČ, address, subscription status, or any other column;
--   - the body is a single fixed `SELECT ... WHERE <external_id_column> =
--     $1 LIMIT 1` — an exact-match lookup on a caller-supplied external id.
--     Both `stripe_customer_id` and `gopay_payer_id` are unique,
--     provider-issued identifiers the caller cannot use to enumerate or
--     dump rows, only to ask "which tenant owns exactly this one external
--     id";
--   - `LANGUAGE sql` (not `plpgsql`) keeps the body a single static
--     statement with a bound parameter — nothing user-controlled is ever
--     concatenated into SQL text, so there is no injection surface, and no
--     procedural logic that could be coerced into doing more than the one
--     SELECT;
--   - `SET search_path = public` pins name resolution so a caller cannot
--     hijack the function via a hostile `search_path`;
--   - `EXECUTE` is revoked from `PUBLIC` and granted only to the app's
--     runtime role (`faktur_app`) — no other role can call it at all.
--
-- Net effect vs. `channel_links`: that exemption removed RLS protection
-- from an entire table, for every possible query against it, forever. This
-- fix leaves `tenants`' RLS fully intact — ordinary SELECT/UPDATE/DELETE
-- against `tenants` through `faktur_app` are still blocked exactly as
-- before — and opens exactly one new, narrow, single-purpose door: "given
-- this external billing id, which tenant id does it belong to." Nothing
-- else about `tenants` becomes readable through it.
CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION find_tenant_id_by_stripe_customer_id(p_stripe_customer_id text)
RETURNS uuid
LANGUAGE sql
SECURITY DEFINER
SET search_path = public
AS $$
  SELECT id FROM tenants WHERE stripe_customer_id = p_stripe_customer_id LIMIT 1;
$$;

REVOKE ALL ON FUNCTION find_tenant_id_by_stripe_customer_id(text) FROM PUBLIC;
GRANT EXECUTE ON FUNCTION find_tenant_id_by_stripe_customer_id(text) TO faktur_app;

CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION find_tenant_id_by_gopay_payer_id(p_gopay_payer_id text)
RETURNS uuid
LANGUAGE sql
SECURITY DEFINER
SET search_path = public
AS $$
  SELECT id FROM tenants WHERE gopay_payer_id = p_gopay_payer_id LIMIT 1;
$$;

REVOKE ALL ON FUNCTION find_tenant_id_by_gopay_payer_id(text) FROM PUBLIC;
GRANT EXECUTE ON FUNCTION find_tenant_id_by_gopay_payer_id(text) TO faktur_app;

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- SECURITY DEFINER lookup function — cross-tenant scheduled-job enumeration
-- (RecurringInvoiceGenerationService)
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Same chicken-and-egg shape as the two functions immediately above, for a
-- different caller: `src/recurring-invoices/recurring-invoice-generation.
-- service.ts` runs a daily job that must scan EVERY tenant's
-- `recurring_invoice_templates` for due rows, which means it needs the full
-- list of tenant ids before any single `app.current_tenant_id` can be set —
-- there is no external key to look up here (unlike the Stripe/GoPay
-- functions above), just "give me every tenant id there is."
--
-- Hardened the same way as `find_tenant_id_by_stripe_customer_id` /
-- `find_tenant_id_by_gopay_payer_id`:
--   - returns ONLY the `id` column — never company name, IČO/DIČ, address,
--     bank details, or subscription status;
--   - `LANGUAGE sql`, a single static statement, no procedural logic;
--   - `SET search_path = public` pins name resolution;
--   - `EXECUTE` revoked from `PUBLIC`, granted only to `faktur_app`.
--
-- Once the job has a tenant id from this list, every subsequent read/write
-- for that tenant goes through the normal `sql.begin()` +
-- `set_config('app.current_tenant_id', ...)` pattern — RLS is fully back in
-- force per tenant for the actual template/invoice queries. This function
-- is deliberately the ONLY place in the app that enumerates every tenant at
-- once; do not reuse it as a shortcut for anything that could instead
-- resolve one specific tenant id.
CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION list_tenant_ids()
RETURNS SETOF uuid
LANGUAGE sql
SECURITY DEFINER
SET search_path = public
AS $$
  SELECT id FROM tenants;
$$;

REVOKE ALL ON FUNCTION list_tenant_ids() FROM PUBLIC;
GRANT EXECUTE ON FUNCTION list_tenant_ids() TO faktur_app;

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- SECURITY DEFINER read view — the no-auth client-portal invoice lookup
-- (blueprint client-portal / email-reminder features; invoices.public_token)
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- This is the THIRD instance of the same recurring shape in this file:
--   1. `channel_links` — an entire table exempted from RLS outright, because
--      its whole job is pre-tenant-context resolution and it holds no
--      sensitive data beyond what its own linking-token/unguessable-id
--      protections already cover (see that comment block above).
--   2. `find_tenant_id_by_stripe_customer_id` / `find_tenant_id_by_gopay_
--      payer_id` — `tenants` legitimately holds sensitive data, so instead
--      of exempting the table, two narrow `SECURITY DEFINER` functions open
--      single-purpose, single-row, single-column doors through otherwise-
--      intact RLS.
--   3. `get_public_invoice_view`, here — the client portal reads an invoice
--      by `public_token` with NO tenant context established yet (a client
--      clicking an emailed link has no session, no JWT, nothing that could
--      populate `app.current_tenant_id`), and `invoices` (like `tenants`)
--      holds real, sensitive, tenant-owned data — so, again, a blanket RLS
--      exemption on the whole table would be wrong. `invoices` keeps
--      `ENABLE`/`FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY` fully intact; this function is
--      the one narrow, purpose-built door through it, following exactly the
--      same hardening rules as the two functions above:
--   - returns ONLY the columns a client actually needs to view/pay their
--     own invoice — never `source`, `ai_confidence_score`,
--     `issued_by_user_id`, `issued_at`, `ai_draft_reviewed_at`,
--     `needs_attention`, or `raw_ai_payload` (all internal drafting/
--     liability/AI-provenance metadata a client has no reason to see, and
--     `raw_ai_payload` in particular could contain a raw AI transcript);
--     `sent_at` / `last_reminder_sent_at` / `reminder_count` are likewise
--     internal dunning-process bookkeeping, not something to expose back to
--     the person being reminded;
--   - `variable_symbol` IS included deliberately: in CZ/SK bank transfers
--     the variable symbol is how the payer's bank identifies which invoice
--     a payment is for, so the client portal cannot function as a "how do I
--     pay this" page without it;
--   - note `subtotal`/`vat_total`/`total` are NOT stored as columns on
--     `invoices` at all (they're derived from `invoice_line_items`), so
--     they are intentionally omitted here rather than guessed at — a
--     future line-items-aware public accessor (if the client portal needs
--     a computed total) should be its own similarly-narrow, similarly-
--     reviewed function, not folded silently into this one;
--   - the body is a single fixed `SELECT ... WHERE public_token = $1 LIMIT
--     1` — an exact-match lookup on a caller-supplied, unguessable random
--     uuid (see `invoices.publicToken` in `drizzle/schema.ts`), not
--     something that can be used to enumerate or dump rows;
--   - `LANGUAGE sql` (not `plpgsql`) keeps the body a single static
--     statement with a bound parameter — no injection surface, no
--     procedural logic that could be coerced into doing more;
--   - `SET search_path = public` pins name resolution against a hostile
--     `search_path`;
--   - `EXECUTE` is revoked from `PUBLIC` and granted only to `faktur_app`.
--
-- Column types below are copied verbatim from `drizzle/schema.ts`'s
-- `invoices` table, NOT from the illustrative sketch this function's spec
-- was originally described with: `issue_date`/`due_date` are declared there
-- as `timestamp("...", { withTimezone: true, mode: "date" })`, which is a
-- real Postgres `timestamptz` column (Drizzle's `mode: "date"` only affects
-- the JS-side value shape, not the underlying SQL type) — so the return
-- type here is `timestamptz`, not `date`, to match what's actually in the
-- database.
CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION get_public_invoice_view(p_public_token uuid)
RETURNS TABLE (
  id uuid,
  tenant_id uuid,
  invoice_number varchar(32),
  variable_symbol varchar(10),
  issue_date timestamptz,
  due_date timestamptz,
  currency varchar(3),
  status text
)
LANGUAGE sql
SECURITY DEFINER
SET search_path = public
AS $$
  SELECT
    id,
    tenant_id,
    invoice_number,
    variable_symbol,
    issue_date,
    due_date,
    currency,
    status::text
  FROM invoices
  WHERE public_token = p_public_token
  LIMIT 1;
$$;

REVOKE ALL ON FUNCTION get_public_invoice_view(uuid) FROM PUBLIC;
GRANT EXECUTE ON FUNCTION get_public_invoice_view(uuid) TO faktur_app;

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- SECURITY DEFINER read functions — client-portal supplementary data
-- (line items + payment/supplier details), keyed by the same public_token as
-- get_public_invoice_view above (apps/backend/src/public-invoices module)
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- A bare invoice header isn't a useful client-portal page on its own: the
-- client needs the itemized line items (to see what they're being charged
-- for and to compute a total, since `invoices` has no stored subtotal/vat/
-- total columns — see the note on that above) and the "who/where to pay"
-- bank details for the SPAYD "scan to pay" QR section. Both of the
-- following follow exactly the same hardening rules as
-- `get_public_invoice_view`: a single fixed `SELECT ... WHERE ... =
-- p_public_token` statement (no procedural logic, no string
-- concatenation), `LANGUAGE sql`, `SET search_path = public`, and
-- `EXECUTE` revoked from `PUBLIC` and granted only to `faktur_app`. Neither
-- function accepts or requires a tenant_id from the caller — the join back
-- to the owning tenant/invoice happens entirely inside the function body,
-- scoped by the caller-supplied unguessable public_token — so application
-- code never needs to hold or pass a tenant_id for this flow at all.
--
-- get_public_invoice_line_items: joins to `invoices` ONLY to filter by
-- public_token; nothing from `invoices` itself is returned here (that's
-- `get_public_invoice_view`'s job). None of `invoice_line_items`' columns
-- are sensitive — they're exactly what already appears on the invoice
-- PDF/ISDOC export the client would otherwise receive by email.
CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION get_public_invoice_line_items(p_public_token uuid)
RETURNS TABLE (
  description text,
  quantity numeric(12,3),
  unit_price numeric(14,2),
  vat_rate numeric(5,2),
  sort_order integer
)
LANGUAGE sql
SECURITY DEFINER
SET search_path = public
AS $$
  SELECT
    ili.description,
    ili.quantity,
    ili.unit_price,
    ili.vat_rate,
    ili.sort_order
  FROM invoice_line_items ili
  JOIN invoices i ON i.id = ili.invoice_id
  WHERE i.public_token = p_public_token
  ORDER BY ili.sort_order;
$$;

REVOKE ALL ON FUNCTION get_public_invoice_line_items(uuid) FROM PUBLIC;
GRANT EXECUTE ON FUNCTION get_public_invoice_line_items(uuid) TO faktur_app;

-- get_public_invoice_supplier_info: joins `invoices` to `tenants` ONLY to
-- filter by public_token (never by a caller-supplied tenant_id — the
-- caller never has one to supply), and returns ONLY the four `tenants`
-- columns a "scan to pay" QR section needs: the supplier's public-facing
-- company name (already printed on every invoice PDF a client would
-- otherwise receive by email) and the three bank-account columns
-- (`bank_account_iban`/`_number`/`_bank_code`). It deliberately does NOT
-- return `legal_name`, `ico`, `dic`, `address_*`, `subscription_status`,
-- `stripe_customer_id`, or `gopay_payer_id` — none of those are needed to
-- render a payment QR code, and the latter three in particular are exactly
-- the kind of internal/billing-provider data this function's siblings
-- above are careful to keep out of the public-portal surface.
CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION get_public_invoice_supplier_info(p_public_token uuid)
RETURNS TABLE (
  company_name text,
  bank_account_iban text,
  bank_account_number text,
  bank_account_bank_code varchar(4)
)
LANGUAGE sql
SECURITY DEFINER
SET search_path = public
AS $$
  SELECT
    t.company_name,
    t.bank_account_iban,
    t.bank_account_number,
    t.bank_account_bank_code
  FROM tenants t
  JOIN invoices i ON i.tenant_id = t.id
  WHERE i.public_token = p_public_token
  LIMIT 1;
$$;

REVOKE ALL ON FUNCTION get_public_invoice_supplier_info(uuid) FROM PUBLIC;
GRANT EXECUTE ON FUNCTION get_public_invoice_supplier_info(uuid) TO faktur_app;

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- SECURITY DEFINER lookup function — resolving a tenant by API key hash
-- BEFORE app.current_tenant_id is known (ApiKeysService.validateKey)
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- `api_keys` (public-API credentials, see `drizzle/schema.ts`) has
-- `ENABLE`/`FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY` with the same `tenant_id =
-- current_setting('app.current_tenant_id', true)::uuid` policy as every
-- other tenant-owned table above. That's exactly right for ordinary queries
-- against `api_keys` (a tenant listing/revoking its own keys) but it hits
-- the identical chicken-and-egg problem the `find_tenant_id_by_*` functions
-- above solve for `tenants`: authenticating an inbound public-API request
-- via `Authorization: Bearer sk_live_...` means looking a key up BY ITS HASH
-- to *discover* which tenant it belongs to — that lookup necessarily happens
-- BEFORE `app.current_tenant_id` can be set, because resolving it is the
-- entire point of the call.
--
-- Fixed the same way, for the same reason: `api_keys` keeps `ENABLE`/`FORCE
-- ROW LEVEL SECURITY` fully intact (unlike the `channel_links` exemption —
-- an API key is a real bearer credential, not a public lookup index, so a
-- blanket RLS exemption would be the wrong fix), and this narrow `SECURITY
-- DEFINER` function opens exactly one purpose-built door through it:
--   - returns ONLY `id` and `tenant_id` — never `name`, `key_prefix`,
--     `key_hash`, or `scopes`;
--   - the `WHERE key_hash = ... AND revoked_at IS NULL` filter means a
--     revoked key and a never-issued/unknown key are INDISTINGUISHABLE to
--     the caller (both return zero rows) — `ApiKeyAuthGuard`
--     (apps/backend/src/auth/api-key-auth.guard.ts) rejects both identically
--     with 401, which is deliberate: a revoked key must not leak "this was
--     once valid" via a different response than an outright unknown one;
--   - the caller-supplied `p_key_hash` is a SHA-256 hex digest computed from
--     the bearer token (see `hashApiKey` in
--     apps/backend/src/api-keys/api-key.util.ts) — never the raw key itself
--     — so this is a single exact-match lookup, not an enumeration surface;
--   - `LANGUAGE sql`, a single static statement, `SET search_path = public`,
--     and `EXECUTE` revoked from `PUBLIC`/granted only to `faktur_app` —
--     identical hardening to every other function in this file.
CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION find_active_api_key_by_hash(p_key_hash text)
RETURNS TABLE (
  id uuid,
  tenant_id uuid
)
LANGUAGE sql
SECURITY DEFINER
SET search_path = public
AS $$
  SELECT id, tenant_id
  FROM api_keys
  WHERE key_hash = p_key_hash
    AND revoked_at IS NULL
  LIMIT 1;
$$;

REVOKE ALL ON FUNCTION find_active_api_key_by_hash(text) FROM PUBLIC;
GRANT EXECUTE ON FUNCTION find_active_api_key_by_hash(text) TO faktur_app;


-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- SECURITY DEFINER bootstrap function — creating the FIRST tenant for a
-- newly-registered user (TenantsService.signup() / app/api/auth/register)
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Same category of chicken-and-egg problem as every function above, at its
-- most literal: a brand-new user has no `tenant_memberships` row at all yet,
-- so there is no `tenant_id` to `SET LOCAL app.current_tenant_id` to before
-- the very INSERT that would create one. `tenants`, `tenant_memberships`,
-- and `tos_acceptances` all correctly keep `ENABLE`/`FORCE ROW LEVEL
-- SECURITY` intact for every other code path — this function opens exactly
-- one purpose-built, atomic door: given a `users.id` that must already
-- exist (the caller — `apps/frontend/app/api/auth/register/route.ts` —
-- creates the user first, in the same transaction), it creates one tenant,
-- one 'owner' `tenant_memberships` row for that user, and one
-- `tos_acceptances` row recording the ToS version the signup form gated on
-- (blueprint §9's signup gate) — then returns the new tenant id. It does
-- NOT accept or touch any other tenant's data, does not grant broader
-- access, and cannot be used to join an EXISTING tenant (there is no path
-- here to attach a user to a tenant they didn't just create — that's a
-- separate, not-yet-built "invite a teammate" feature).
--
-- `content_hash` on the `tos_versions` row is a clearly-provisional
-- placeholder (`md5('placeholder:' || version)`) since there is no real,
-- attorney-reviewed ToS text yet to hash (see
-- `app/[locale]/legal/terms/page.tsx` — still a structural placeholder) —
-- swap this for a real content hash once real text is published.
CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION bootstrap_tenant_for_user(
  p_user_id uuid,
  p_company_name text,
  p_tos_version text,
  p_ip_address text DEFAULT NULL
)
RETURNS uuid
LANGUAGE plpgsql
SECURITY DEFINER
SET search_path = public
AS $$
DECLARE
  v_tenant_id uuid;
  v_tos_version_id uuid;
BEGIN
  IF p_company_name IS NULL OR btrim(p_company_name) = '' THEN
    RAISE EXCEPTION 'company name is required';
  END IF;

  INSERT INTO tos_versions (version, content_hash)
  VALUES (p_tos_version, md5('placeholder:' || p_tos_version))
  ON CONFLICT (version) DO NOTHING;

  SELECT id INTO v_tos_version_id FROM tos_versions WHERE version = p_tos_version;

  INSERT INTO tenants (company_name, legal_name)
  VALUES (btrim(p_company_name), btrim(p_company_name))
  RETURNING id INTO v_tenant_id;

  INSERT INTO tenant_memberships (tenant_id, user_id, role)
  VALUES (v_tenant_id, p_user_id, 'owner');

  INSERT INTO tos_acceptances (user_id, tenant_id, tos_version_id, ip_address)
  VALUES (p_user_id, v_tenant_id, v_tos_version_id, p_ip_address);

  RETURN v_tenant_id;
END;
$$;

REVOKE ALL ON FUNCTION bootstrap_tenant_for_user(uuid, text, text, text) FROM PUBLIC;
GRANT EXECUTE ON FUNCTION bootstrap_tenant_for_user(uuid, text, text, text) TO faktur_app;
